<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Datasets Descriptions</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure comprehensive assessment across various styles of questions and knowledge scopes, we evaluate our method on three widely used open-domain QA datasets: SQuAD 2.0<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, TriviaQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and WikiQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SQuAD 2.0</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>: A large-scale benchmark consisting of over 100,000 questions derived from Wikipedia articles.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TriviaQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>: A large-scale reading comprehension dataset containing 650K question-answer-evidence triples, featuring 95K trivia enthusiast-authored questions paired with independently sourced evidence documents for distant supervision.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WikiQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>: A dataset of real Bing query logs paired with candidate answer sentences from Wikipedia, aimed at evaluating answer sentence selection performance under realistic user question distribution.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For each dataset, we sample a subset of factual queries and assign: (1) a correct answer <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{{true}}</annotation></semantics></math>, directly verifiable in model memory or retrieved evidence; (2) a fake target answer <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is plausible but incorrect. The attacker’s goal is to craft adversarial external knowledge <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math> that causes the model to return <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{{true}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Theoretical Justification of Neuron-Guided Poisoning</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our hypothesis is that overriding an LLM’s internal factual knowledge via external knowledge is causally linked to the activation of certain internal units.
We define these units as the Poison-Responsiveness Score.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> be a query, <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{{true}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the ground-truth answer, and <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> the adversary-specified target. Let the LLM’s output for a given input <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be a token-level probability distribution <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pr(a|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pr(a|x)</annotation></semantics></math> computed via a softmax over the final representation <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pr(a|x)=softmax(Wf(x))_{a}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pr(a|x)=softmax(Wf(x))_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output head and <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> depends on internal neuron activations. The attacker’s goal is to construct the external knowledge <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> such that the model predicts <math id="A2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{{true}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(q,e)\to\hat{a}\neq a^{{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(q,e)\to\hat{a}\neq a^{{true}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The attribution for each neuron is given by <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Poison-Responsiveness Score. ‣ 3.2 Poison-Responsive Neuron ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ NeuroGenPoisoning: Neuron-Guided Attacks on Retrieval-Augmented Generation of LLM via Genetic Optimization of External Knowledge" class="ltx_ref">Equation <span class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B22222;">(<span class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></span>)</span></a>. In our approach, we chose to use the absolute values of <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IG</annotation></semantics></math> to quantify overall attribution strength, agnostic to positive or negative influence. We define <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(e)</annotation></semantics></math> as the sum of Integrated Gradients (IG) attribution over a global set of top-<math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> neurons <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{{top}-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{{top}-r}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(e)=\sum_{(l,i)\in\mathcal{N}_{{top}-h}}|{IG}_{(l,i)}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(e)=\sum_{(l,i)\in\mathcal{N}_{{top}-h}}|{IG}_{(l,i)}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{l,i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{l,i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the activation of the <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th neuron in the <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer. Then <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of all internal neurons:</p>
<table id="A2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=g\left(\{f_{l,i}(x)\}_{l,i}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=g\left(\{f_{l,i}(x)\}_{l,i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We define the model’s original belief as:</p>
<table id="A2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(q,e)=Pr(a^{{true}}|q,e)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(q,e)=Pr(a^{{true}}|q,e)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly, the confidence of the fake answer can be defined as:</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}(q,e)=Pr(\hat{a}|q,e)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}(q,e)=Pr(\hat{a}|q,e)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">We consider the total derivative of the output probability with respect to each neuron:</p>
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\hat{K}(q,e)}{\partial f_{l,i}(x)}=\frac{\partial Pr(\hat{a}|x)}{\partial f(x)}\cdot\frac{\partial f(x)}{\partial f_{l,i}(x)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\hat{K}(q,e)}{\partial f_{l,i}(x)}=\frac{\partial Pr(\hat{a}|x)}{\partial f(x)}\cdot\frac{\partial f(x)}{\partial f_{l,i}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.5" class="ltx_p">The relationship between neuron activation and output probability can be derived via the chain rule:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\hat{K}}{dx}=\sum_{(l,i)}\frac{\partial\hat{K}}{\partial f_{l,i}}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{l,i}}{\partial x}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\hat{K}}{dx}=\sum_{(l,i)}\frac{\partial\hat{K}}{\partial f_{l,i}}\cdot\frac{\partial f_{l,i}}{\partial x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the performance proxy predicted in a poisoned configuration; <math id="A2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{l,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{l,i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the activation of the neuron <math id="A2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the layer <math id="A2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a function of the input <math id="A2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="A2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\hat{K}}{dx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\hat{K}}{dx}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total derivative that captures how perturbations in the input <math id="A2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> affect the final output of the model. By applying the chain rule, the derivative is decomposed into two multiplicative components: (1) <math id="A2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f_{l,i}}{\partial x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f_{l,i}}{\partial x}</annotation></semantics></math>: captures the level of tolerance of the activation of a neuron to changes in input <math id="A2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. This is what Integrated Gradients (IG) estimates; (2) <math id="A2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\hat{K}}{\partial f_{l,i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\hat{K}}{\partial f_{l,i}}</annotation></semantics></math>: measures how activation of a neuron <math id="A2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{l,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{l,i}</annotation></semantics></math> affects the final output of models <math id="A2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. It emphasizes the importance of that neuron for the model’s prediction. Thus, neuron activations can modulate the probability of output token. When <math id="A2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e)</annotation></semantics></math> increases, its influence on model output increases. shifting probability mass toward <math id="A2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and away from <math id="A2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{{true}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The knowledge override shift can be computed as:</p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\hat{K}_{t}=K(q,e_{t})-K(q,e_{0})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\hat{K}_{t}=K(q,e_{t})-K(q,e_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, we obtain the positive correlation: <math id="A2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\hat{K}_{t}\propto\Delta P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\hat{K}_{t}\propto\Delta P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that as the Poison-Responsive Score increases, the confidence in the adversary’s answer increases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Exploration of the Number of Poison-Responsive Neurons</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our main experiments, we set the number of Poison-Responsive Neurons <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> to 10. To evaluate the robustness of our method with respect to this hyperparameter, we conduct a series of experiments that vary <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[5,15]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[5,15]</annotation></semantics></math>. The results of different settings are shown in <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix C Exploration of the Number of Poison-Responsive Neurons ‣ NeuroGenPoisoning: Neuron-Guided Attacks on Retrieval-Augmented Generation of LLM via Genetic Optimization of External Knowledge" class="ltx_ref">Table <span class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B22222;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span></a>. We did not observe a significant difference in the different settings of the value of <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Effect of varying the number of top-<math id="A3.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> Poison-Responsive Neurons on final Population Overwrite Success Rate (POSR)</figcaption>
